<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="chapter-infinite-series" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Infinite Series
  </title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In this chapter, we explore the convergence and divergence of infinite series of real numbers and real-valued functions. After establishing several sufficient criteria for convergence, we will investigate <term> power series </term>. The most productive source of power series is <xref ref="theorem-taylors-theorem" text="title"/>, which when applied to smooth functions produces the partial sums of a <term> Taylor series </term>. We will use such Taylor series to analyze <term> analytic functions </term>, which are locally equal to their Taylor series.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <xi:include href="./section-partial-sums-and-infinite-series/section-partial-sums-and-infinite-series.ptx"/>

  <xi:include href="./section-tests-for-series-convergence/section-tests-for-series-convergence.ptx"/>

  <xi:include href="./section-power-series/section-power-series.ptx"/>

  <xi:include href="./section-analytic-functions/section-analytic-functions.ptx"/>

  <conclusion>
